    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q67-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}=(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}=(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2$, for $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$.\n\nMultiply the numerator and denominator by $1+\\sin\\theta$, then use $1-\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos^2\\theta$.",
          "worked": "For $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)^2}{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)}\\\\\n\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)^2}{1-\\sin^2\\theta}\\\\\n\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)^2\\\\\n\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}&=(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q67-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $\\theta$ be in the first quadrant. Write down the simplest expression for $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta$.",
          "worked": "In the first quadrant, $\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta>0$. Using part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=\\sqrt{(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q67-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find a similar expression for when $\\theta$ is in the second quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-\\sec\\theta-\\tan\\theta$.",
          "worked": "In the second quadrant, $\\cos\\theta<0$ and $1+\\sin\\theta>0$, so $\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}<0$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=|\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta|\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=-\\sec\\theta-\\tan\\theta.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q67-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Write down a general result for $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}$ that is true regardless of the quadrant in which $\\theta$ lies.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}=|\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta|$, for $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$.\n\nUse $\\sqrt{u^2}=|u|$.",
          "worked": "Using part (a) and $\\sqrt{u^2}=|u|$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=\\sqrt{(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}}&=|\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta|.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThis holds in every quadrant wherever both sides are defined, namely $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
